==Old English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|skrīþaną|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|(s)kreit-|lang=ine-pro}}, {{recons|(s)ker-||twist, turn, bend|lang=ine-pro}}.  Germanic cognates include Old Saxon ''[[scriðan|scrīðan]]'' (Dutch ''[[schrijden]]''), Old High German ''scrîtan'' (German ''[[schreiten]]''), Old Norse ''skríða'' ‘creep, glide, [[scree]]’ (Swedish ''[[skrida]]'').  The Indo-European root also gave Latin ''[[crisare]]'' and Middle Irish {{term|crith|lang=mga}} (Breton ''[[skrija]]'' ‘tremble with fear’).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈʃriːθɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|type=strong|class=I|head=scrīþan}}

# to [[move]], [[glide]], [[wander]]

